  <article aria-label="The origins of the Romanians">
    <p>Romanian is a Romance language, but a language family is not a forwarding address: its Latin ancestry does not tell us precisely where its speakers’ ancestors lived through every century. The central dispute concerns whether Romanized communities survived north of the Danube after Rome abandoned its province of Dacia, whether Romanian developed principally farther south before spreading north, or whether both processes contributed. Even the map needs a warning label, because Roman Dacia, conquered under Trajan in 106, covered only part of present-day Romania, while Dobruja belonged to a different Roman provincial setting and remained under imperial authority much longer. Emperor Aurelian’s withdrawal from Dacia, conventionally dated within 271–275, removed the provincial administration and army and transferred people southward, but its demographic completeness is disputed. Nor were ancient Roman provincials already Romanians in the modern national sense: a medieval language community and, much later, a nation emerged through prolonged historical change. The useful question is therefore not whether an entire modern people stayed put, but where Latin-speaking populations persisted, moved, and became something new.</p>

    <p>The Daco-Roman continuity hypothesis proposes that a substantial part of Dacia’s Romanized population remained after the imperial evacuation and contributed to the later Romanian-speaking population north of the river. Its strongest starting point is an ordinary historical consideration: an administration can leave more neatly than a countryside, whose inhabitants have fields, families, and limited enthusiasm for official relocation schemes. Supporters cite evidence of continued occupation at some sites, Roman-derived objects and practices after the withdrawal, and proposed survivals of ancient place and river names. Each category requires care, however, because a reused building does not disclose its occupant’s language, an imported object can travel without a population, and a river name can pass between linguistic communities. Versions that imagine an isolated people preserving almost unchanged Latin in mountain refuges also understate the extensive contacts visible in Romanian itself. Continuity is thus a defensible historical hypothesis, but neither continued settlement nor Romanian’s Latin vocabulary alone proves an uninterrupted Romanian-speaking presence throughout all the territories of modern Romania.</p>

    <p>The principal immigrationist hypothesis instead places the main development of the Romanians’ linguistic ancestors south of the Danube, among the Romanized populations of the Balkans, followed by movement into lands farther north. Associated especially with the nineteenth-century scholar Robert Roesler, though neither invented by him nor uniform in its proposed chronology, it is not a theory that Romanian originated with French merchants or with Slavs adopting a few Latin words. Its advocates emphasize the longer duration of Roman rule in the southern Balkans, gaps in explicit northern written testimony, and Romanian’s relationships with other Eastern Romance varieties, including Aromanian. Romanian–Albanian lexical correspondences and shared Balkan linguistic features also enter the argument, although their dating and geographical implications remain disputed. Medieval references to Vlachs—an external designation often used for Romance-speaking populations—help establish particular communities’ presence, but cannot automatically identify every community’s origins or the date of its arrival. The major difficulty is turning these indications of Balkan connections into proof of an exclusively southern homeland and a sufficiently documented northward migration.</p>

    <p>A third family of explanations treats the Danube less as a civilizational wall than as a corridor that could be crossed in both directions, even when governments preferred otherwise. Such models combine survival in some northern areas with continuing interaction, population movement, and linguistic development across a broader southeastern European zone. They accommodate a basic distinction often lost in polemic: local continuity and immigration can happen together, and neither requires ethnic or biological purity. Romanian’s inherited Latin structure, substantial Slavic influence, and relationships with neighboring languages establish a history of contact, but do not provide a uniquely decipherable itinerary. Archaeology can illuminate settlement and exchange, while ancient DNA can investigate ancestry and mobility; neither directly identifies the language spoken by an otherwise undocumented community. These approaches make a connected regional history plausible, without supplying a universally accepted answer about the relative northern and southern contributions to Romanian ethnogenesis.</p>

    <p>The dispute became especially combustible in Transylvania, where arguments about antiquity intersected with inequalities of political status and, later, competing national territorial projects. Eighteenth-century Romanian intellectuals associated with the Transylvanian School emphasized Roman ancestry and historical rootedness in campaigns for recognition, arguments reflected in the petition known as the <em>Supplex Libellus Valachorum</em> of 1791. Some Hungarian and Austrian writers, conversely, used late-arrival accounts to weaken Romanian claims to precedence or entitlement, particularly in the increasingly nationalized controversies of the nineteenth century. Neither a scholar’s nationality nor a theory’s political usefulness settles whether its evidence is sound, but both help explain why an ambiguous artifact could acquire the responsibilities of a border guard. Romanian communist historiography changed over time: early Soviet-oriented accounts stressed Slavic connections, whereas later national communism elevated indigenous antiquity and continuity, sometimes with extravagant emphasis on the Dacians. These rival appropriations share a faulty premise whenever they imply that modern civil rights or legitimate political membership depend on whose ancestors can produce the oldest receipt.</p>

    <p>The secure conclusion is that Romanian developed from the Latin spoken in southeastern Europe, through centuries of transformation and contact, rather than appearing fully formed when Trajan crossed the Danube. Romanized populations certainly existed on both sides of the river during antiquity, but the later distribution and movements of the communities ancestral to Romanian speakers remain incompletely documented. The end of Roman provincial government in Dacia is established; the disappearance of every Latin-speaking inhabitant is not. Equally, acknowledging possible northern survival does not establish continuous Romanian occupation of every later Romanian region or eliminate the importance of migration from the Balkans. The most responsible account presents the competing geographical models, distinguishes evidence from inference, and resists announcing a tidy consensus where substantial disagreement survives. Romanians need no miraculous exemption from the migrations and mixtures of European history to have a history of their own.</p>
