### ID-only service

```java
@Service
public class AccountService {
    private final List<AccountLookup> lookups;

    public AccountService(List<AccountLookup> lookups) {
        this.lookups = lookups;
    }

    @Transactional(readOnly = true)
    public Account getById(Integer id) {
        Objects.requireNonNull(id, "id must not be null");

        for (AccountLookup lookup : lookups) {
            Optional<Account> account = lookup.findById(id);
            if (account.isPresent()) {
                return account.get();
            }
        }

        throw new EntityNotFoundException("Account not found: " + id);
    }
}
```

Adding a new account type requires its entity, repository, and `AccountLookup` component—not changes to this service.

**Important limitations:**

- This assumes IDs are globally unique across all account tables, as you stated. Separate table primary keys do not enforce that invariant; use an appropriate shared ID-allocation mechanism or central account table.
- A lookup can execute one query per account type. For many types, a central account/type directory or shared account table is preferable.
- Return a safe DTO from controllers rather than serializing account entities containing password hashes.

