## My answer to the prompt

Your biggest memory cost is storing **26 pointers at every node**, including leaves. On a typical 64-bit system, those pointers alone take 208 bytes. Each node also has a `vector` object and a separate allocation for its child pointers.

A good improvement is to store all nodes in a `vector` and use **32-bit child indices instead of pointers**. This also automatically frees the trie when the function returns; your current code leaks every allocated node.

```cpp
#include <array>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
    struct Node {
        array<int, 26> child{}; // 0 = absent; root is never a child
        int cnt = 0;
    };

public:
    vector<int> sumPrefixScores(vector<string>& words) {
        vector<Node> nodes(1); // root at index 0

        for (const string& word : words) {
            int cur = 0;

            for (char ch : word) {
                int c = ch - 'a';
                int next = nodes[cur].child[c];

                if (next == 0) {
                    next = static_cast<int>(nodes.size());
                    nodes[cur].child[c] = next;
                    nodes.emplace_back();
                }

                // Indices remain valid even if emplace_back reallocates.
                cur = next;
                ++nodes[cur].cnt;
            }
        }

        vector<int> answer;
        answer.reserve(words.size());

        for (const string& word : words) {
            int cur = 0;
            int score = 0;

            for (char ch : word) {
                cur = nodes[cur].child[ch - 'a'];
                score += nodes[cur].cnt;
            }

            answer.push_back(score);
        }

        return answer;
    }
};
```

This assumes lowercase `a`–`z`, that the node count fits in an `int`, and that scores fit in an `int`, as in your original code.

**Memory improvements:**
- Typically **108 bytes per node**, rather than roughly 240 bytes plus allocator overhead.
- No separate child-array allocation or individual `new` per node.
- No recursive call stack.
- No temporary string copies in the loops.

**Caveat:** A growing `vector` has unused capacity and temporarily needs both old and new storage during reallocation. Calling `reserve()` with a good node-count estimate can reduce that peak. Reserving `1 + sum(word lengths)` is a safe upper bound, but can waste substantial memory when words share prefixes.

If you still need less memory, store only existing edges—for example, in a pooled sparse adjacency structure—rather than reserving 26 child slots per node.

