## My answer to the prompt

A common approach is to read one `size_t` at a time and test whether it contains a zero byte:

```c
(w - ones) & ~w & highs
```

For 8-bit bytes, `ones` has `0x01` in every byte and `highs` has `0x80` in every byte. The result is nonzero exactly when some byte is zero.

However, **word-sized `size_t` alone does not make this a portable replacement for `strlen`**. A word load may read past the string’s allocated object, and casting a character pointer to `size_t *` can violate alignment and effective-type/aliasing rules.

Here is a word-at-a-time implementation for a **padded-buffer contract**: at least `sizeof(size_t) - 1` additional readable bytes must follow the terminator.

```c
#include <stddef.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <limits.h>
#include <string.h>

_Static_assert(CHAR_BIT == 8, "Requires 8-bit bytes");

size_t padded_strlen(const char *s)
{
    const size_t ones  = SIZE_MAX / 255;
    const size_t highs = ones * 128;
    size_t n = 0;

    for (;;) {
        size_t w;
        memcpy(&w, s + n, sizeof w);

        if ((w - ones) & ~w & highs) {
            for (size_t i = 0; i < sizeof w; ++i) {
                if (s[n + i] == '\0')
                    return n + i;
            }
        }

        n += sizeof w;
    }
}
```

Fixed-size `memcpy` commonly compiles to a word load and avoids alignment and aliasing violations. The final byte scan works on either endianness. The padding requirement remains essential: `memcpy` does not legalize out-of-bounds reads.

For an ordinary, unpadded C string, use the platform’s `strlen`, or implement a byte loop if strict portability is required. Optimized library implementations rely on compiler and platform guarantees beyond the assumption in the question. Actual speedups require benchmarking.

