## My answer to the prompt

Use **word-at-a-time scanning**: check an unaligned prefix byte by byte, then load a `size_t` at a time and test whether it contains a zero byte.

For 8-bit bytes, this expression is nonzero exactly when `w` contains a zero byte:

```c
(w - ones) & ~w & highs
```

Here, `ones` has `0x01` in every byte and `highs` has `0x80` in every byte.

However, **`size_t` being word-sized is not sufficient to make arbitrary word loads valid C**. Loads through a cast `size_t *` can violate aliasing rules, and a word load can read beyond the string’s allocated object.

The following illustrates the algorithm using `memcpy` to avoid aliasing violations. It assumes ordinary 8-bit-byte hardware, conventional pointer alignment, and that **every full word copied lies within readable storage in the source object**, including any bytes after the terminator:

```c
#include <stddef.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <string.h>

size_t fast_strlen_padded(const char *s)
{
    const unsigned char *p = (const unsigned char *)s;
    const size_t ones = (size_t)-1 / 255;
    const size_t highs = ones * 128;
    size_t n = 0;

    /* Reach a word boundary, checking every skipped byte. */
    while ((uintptr_t)p % sizeof(size_t) != 0) {
        if (*p == 0)
            return n;
        ++p;
        ++n;
    }

    for (;;) {
        size_t w;
        memcpy(&w, p, sizeof w);

        if ((w - ones) & ~w & highs) {
            /* Inspect memory order: works on either endianness. */
            for (size_t i = 0; i < sizeof w; ++i) {
                if (p[i] == 0)
                    return n + i;
            }
        }

        p += sizeof w;
        n += sizeof w;
    }
}
```

Optimizing compilers commonly turn the fixed-size `memcpy` into a word load. The zero-detection expression answers whether **any** byte is zero; borrow propagation means it is not necessarily an exact mask of all zero bytes.

For a general-purpose application, prefer the library’s `strlen`: optimized implementations handle platform-specific access rules and often use SIMD. Speedup depends on hardware, compiler, and string length—not simply the number of bytes per word.

