Hi! Do you need **the actual CLR memory image for diagnostics**, or **a byte representation you can store/send and reconstruct later**? Those require different approaches.

A `HashSet<string>` is not a contiguous, blittable value: it references arrays, strings, and a comparer. Copying its instance memory would mostly capture process-specific references—not a usable copy of its contents. `Marshal.StructureToPtr` is not suitable for this type.

If you mean a compact byte representation, you can define an explicit binary format without inspecting runtime internals:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.IO;
using System.Text;

public static class SetEncoding
{
    // Format:
    // Int32 element count
    // For each element:
    //   Boolean: whether the element is non-null
    //   If non-null: BinaryWriter's length-prefixed UTF-8 string
    public static byte[] ToBytes(HashSet<string> set)
    {
        if (set == null)
            throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(set));

        using var stream = new MemoryStream();
        using (var writer = new BinaryWriter(
            stream, new UTF8Encoding(false, true), leaveOpen: true))
        {
            writer.Write(set.Count);

            foreach (var value in set)
            {
                writer.Write(value != null);
                if (value != null)
                    writer.Write(value);
            }
        }

        return stream.ToArray();
    }
}

// Usage:
// byte[] bytes = SetEncoding.ToBytes(
//     new HashSet<string> { "apple", "banana" });
```

This is **serialization, not a raw memory dump**. Important details:

- It supports null elements, which a `HashSet<string>` can contain.
- It does not encode the comparer; reconstruction must use the intended comparer.
- Set enumeration order is not guaranteed, so equal sets need not produce identical bytes.
- The strict UTF-8 encoder rejects unpaired UTF-16 surrogates. If you need to preserve every possible C# string exactly, use an explicit UTF-16-code-unit format instead.
- Do not modify the set while serializing it.

If you specifically need a diagnostic memory snapshot, please provide your .NET version, architecture, and intended consumer. There is no supported, portable raw-marshalling recipe for this object graph.

