## My answer to the prompt

Do you need a **raw CLR memory snapshot**, or a **byte representation of the set’s contents** for storage or native interop? Which .NET version and target architecture are you using?

A `HashSet<string>` is not a contiguous block containing its strings. It contains references to arrays, strings, and a comparer. Copying the object’s memory would copy references—not the entire contents—and those bytes could not safely reconstruct the set.

If you want a low-level, explicitly defined binary format, you can write the contents without inspecting CLR internals:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.IO;

public static class SetBytes
{
    // Format (little-endian):
    // Int32 element count
    // For each element:
    //   Int32 UTF-16 code-unit count (-1 means null)
    //   UInt16 code units
    public static byte[] Encode(HashSet<string> set)
    {
        if (set == null)
            throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(set));

        using var stream = new MemoryStream();
        using (var writer = new BinaryWriter(
            stream, System.Text.Encoding.UTF8, leaveOpen: true))
        {
            writer.Write(set.Count);

            foreach (string s in set)
            {
                if (s == null)
                {
                    writer.Write(-1);
                    continue;
                }

                writer.Write(s.Length);
                foreach (char c in s)
                    writer.Write((ushort)c);
            }
        }

        return stream.ToArray();
    }
}
```

This preserves the strings’ exact UTF-16 code units, including null elements, but **not** the hash table’s internal layout or comparer. Supply the intended comparer when reconstructing the set. Enumeration order is not guaranteed, so equal sets need not produce identical bytes; sort first if you require a canonical representation. Do not modify the set during encoding.

If you truly need a heap snapshot, use runtime-aware diagnostic tooling. For native interop, define an explicit buffer format with the native consumer rather than exposing CLR object internals.

