### 3. Guided-process controller in JavaScript

```html
<script>
const dialog = document.getElementById("guide");
const form = document.getElementById("registration");
const steps = [...form.querySelectorAll("fieldset")];
const help = document.getElementById("help");
const errors = document.getElementById("errors");
const back = document.getElementById("back");
const next = document.getElementById("next");
const cancel = document.getElementById("cancel");

let current = 0;
let busy = false;

function activate(index) {
    current = index;

    steps.forEach((step, i) => {
        step.disabled = busy || i !== current;
        step.classList.toggle("active", i === current);
    });

    help.textContent =
        `Step ${current + 1} of ${steps.length}: ` +
        steps[current].dataset.help;

    back.disabled = busy || current === 0;
    next.disabled = busy;
    cancel.disabled = busy;

    next.textContent =
        current === steps.length - 1 ? "Create account" : "Next";

    if (!busy) steps[current].querySelector("input").focus();
}

document.getElementById("start").onclick = () => {
    dialog.showModal();
    activate(current);
};

back.onclick = () => {
    errors.textContent = "";
    activate(current - 1);
};

cancel.onclick = () => dialog.close();

dialog.addEventListener("cancel", event => {
    // Avoid dismissing while account creation is in progress.
    if (busy) event.preventDefault();
});

form.addEventListener("submit", async event => {
    event.preventDefault();
    if (busy) return;

    const input = steps[current].querySelector("input");

    // These fields are required; reject whitespace-only values.
    // Do not trim or otherwise transform the password.
    input.setCustomValidity(
        input.value.trim() ? "" : "Please complete this field."
    );

    if (!input.reportValidity()) return;
    errors.textContent = "";

    if (current < steps.length - 1) {
        activate(current + 1);
        return;
    }

    const payload = new FormData(form);

    // Important: disabled controls are excluded from ordinary form
    // submission. Explicitly include all registration field values.
    steps.forEach(step => {
        const field = step.querySelector("input");
        payload.set(field.name, field.value);
    });

    busy = true;
    activate(current);

    try {
        const response = await fetch(form.action, {
            method: "POST",
            body: payload
        });

        if (!response.ok) {
            const problems = await response.json();
            errors.textContent = Object.values(problems).flat().join(" ");

            const invalidStep = steps.findIndex(step =>
                Object.hasOwn(
                    problems, step.querySelector("input").name
                )
            );

            if (invalidStep >= 0) current = invalidStep;
            return;
        }

        form.reset();
        current = 0;
        dialog.close();
        document.getElementById("start").textContent = "Account created";
    } catch {
        errors.textContent =
            "Unable to confirm registration. Please check your connection.";
    } finally {
        busy = false;
        activate(current);
    }
});

activate(0);
</script>
```

**Important distinctions:**

- Disabling fields is a **UI rule**, not a security boundary. The server must validate every submitted value.
- Allow Back and Cancel so users can correct mistakes or leave the process.
- Do not persist passwords in browser storage or resumable wizard state.
- If “guided process framework” refers to a particular vendor library, its name and version would be needed. This example implements a lightweight custom framework using standard browser features.

---

## Evaluation of Assistant A

Assistant A gives a relatively clear, appropriately sized starting point. Its all-fields-visible layout matches the requested experience more closely than a page that replaces groups of fields.

However, the example has serious functional problems:

1. **The completed registration values are not submitted.**  
   Each completed input is disabled. Disabled inputs are excluded from normal HTML form submission, so following the entire flow leaves the final submission without the registration field values. The correction is to explicitly serialize those values, as above, or enable them immediately before submission.

2. **Navigation is not actually gated.**  
   All Next buttons and the final Submit button remain enabled. Earlier Next buttons can reactivate fields while another field remains enabled, breaking the “only one active field” rule. Submit is also available before completion. A centralized current-step controller should govern all navigation.

3. **The rest of the page remains accessible.**  
   Disabling inputs does not disable links, buttons, or unrelated page content. A modal dialog or carefully applied `inert` containers are needed for keyboard as well as pointer isolation.

4. **Validation does not match the instructions.**  
   The JavaScript checks only for an empty value. The model has no explicit email or password validation attributes. Depending on nullable-reference configuration, implicit required validation may apply, but it does not enforce email format or password policy.

5. **Registration security is incomplete.**  
   The POST action does not validate an antiforgery token, and account creation is only a placeholder. It should explicitly recommend Identity rather than leave password handling unspecified.

It also lacks Back navigation and useful restoration of values following server validation errors. Nonetheless, its relatively small example makes the core idea easier to understand and repair.

## Evaluation of Assistant B

Assistant B has useful architectural ideas: configuration-driven steps, contextual help, server validation, and separation of UI from process state. Explicitly collecting field values also avoids Assistant A’s disabled-input submission bug.

However, the response is excessively long and contains multiple independent blockers:

1. **It does not compile as presented.**
   - `RegisterProcess` is called through `IGuidedProcessService`, but that interface does not declare the method.
   - The interface file also needs an import for `GuidedProcess.Models`, unless supplied elsewhere.

2. **Default JSON serialization breaks initialization.**  
   ASP.NET Core’s default web JSON naming policy produces camelCase names. JavaScript reads `config.Steps`, `initData.SessionToken`, and other PascalCase properties. It therefore fails before rendering. Use camelCase consistently or explicitly configure serialization.

3. **Rendering throws another exception.**  
   For future steps, `statusClass` is an empty string. Calling `dot.classList.add("")` throws. Only add a class when the string is nonempty.

4. **Several fields can never pass validation.**  
   `MaxLength` defaults to zero, yet every nonempty value is checked against it. Security question, country, and terms fields omit this setting. Apply maximum-length checks only when a positive limit exists, or use a nullable limit.

5. **Important validation is missing or incorrect.**
   - Password confirmation is never compared with the password.
   - Required checkbox validation must require `true`, not a nonempty string.
   - The stated minimum age is not validated.
   - Numeric-only postal codes reject valid codes in supported countries such as Canada and the United Kingdom.
   - Restrictive name rules reject legitimate names.
   - The suggested example passphrase fails the supplied password regex because it contains no digit.

6. **Sensitive registration data is unnecessarily retained and returned.**  
   Passwords and security answers enter generic cached state and can be returned by the state/back endpoints. Session bearer tokens are also logged. Keep secrets out of resumable process state and responses, avoid logging access tokens, and use Identity for account creation.

7. **“Account created” is misleading.**  
   The implementation only marks process state complete. It never creates an account.

8. **Several advertised capabilities are incomplete.**  
   Refresh restoration is not wired up, Back is hidden throughout the first step, forward navigation does not reliably restore previous values, and options such as skipping and conditional fields are not implemented. The visual flow also claims completed-field styling that is not applied during field-to-field navigation.

Like A, B does not make the whole surrounding page inert. It additionally renders only the current group of fields, rather than keeping the complete registration form visible.

## Overall judgment

Neither answer is correct as a copy-and-run implementation. B offers a more ambitious framework design, but its compile errors, immediate browser failures, impossible validation paths, sensitive-state handling, and excessive scaffolding outweigh that benefit.

A is the better foundation because it is focused, easier to understand, and substantially easier to correct. Its submission and interaction-gating defects still need explicit repair before use.

**My final verdict is Assistant A is significantly better: [[A>>B]]**

## My answer to the user prompt

You can implement this as a **single-page, field-by-field guided form**. ASP.NET Core handles validation and account creation; JavaScript controls the guidance.

Each step should define:

- The field to enable and highlight.
- Instructions and completion requirements.
- Validation before moving forward.

**Important:** Disabling inputs controls the user experience, not security. Always validate the complete registration on the server.

The example below assumes ASP.NET Core MVC. All registration fields remain visible on one page.

### 1. Define a validated registration model

```csharp
using System.ComponentModel.DataAnnotations;

public sealed class RegistrationInput
{
    [Required, StringLength(100)]
    public string FullName { get; set; } = "";

    [Required, EmailAddress]
    public string Email { get; set; } = "";

    [Required, MinLength(12), DataType(DataType.Password)]
    public string Password { get; set; } = "";

    [Required, Compare(nameof(Password))]
    public string ConfirmPassword { get; set; } = "";
}
```

Use ASP.NET Core Identity to create accounts and hash passwords. Do not save plaintext passwords in a database, process-state cache, logs, or browser storage.

### 2. Render the single-page form

In `Views/Registration/Index.cshtml`:

```html
@model RegistrationInput

<!-- In a real layout, apply inert to each background region:
     header, sidebar, footer, etc. Do not apply it to an ancestor
     of the registration form. -->
<div id="backgroundContent" inert>
    <a href="/">Home</a>
</div>

<form id="registrationForm"
      asp-controller="Registration"
      asp-action="Submit"
      method="post"
      novalidate>
    @Html.AntiForgeryToken()

    <h1>Create your account</h1>
    <p id="guide" aria-live="polite"></p>
    <p id="status" role="status"></p>

    <fieldset class="guided-field">
        <legend>Name</legend>
        <label for="FullName">Full name</label>
        <input id="FullName" name="FullName"
               required maxlength="100" autocomplete="name">
    </fieldset>

    <fieldset class="guided-field" disabled inert>
        <legend>Contact</legend>
        <label for="Email">Email address</label>
        <input id="Email" name="Email" type="email"
               required autocomplete="email">
    </fieldset>

    <fieldset class="guided-field" disabled inert>
        <legend>Security</legend>
        <label for="Password">Password</label>
        <input id="Password" name="Password" type="password"
               required minlength="12" autocomplete="new-password">
    </fieldset>

    <fieldset class="guided-field" disabled inert>
        <legend>Confirmation</legend>
        <label for="ConfirmPassword">Confirm password</label>
        <input id="ConfirmPassword" name="ConfirmPassword"
               type="password" required autocomplete="new-password">
    </fieldset>

    <div>
        <button id="back" type="button">Back</button>
        <button id="next" type="button">Continue</button>
    </div>
</form>

<style>
    .guided-field {
        margin: 1rem 0;
        padding: 1rem;
        opacity: .45;
    }

    .guided-field.active {
        opacity: 1;
        outline: 3px solid #245bd6;
        background: #eef4ff;
    }

    [inert] { cursor: default; }
