```

`disabled` prevents inactive fields from being edited. `inert` additionally prevents interaction and focus on links or other controls within an inactive region. CSS alone, especially `pointer-events: none`, does not block keyboard interaction.

Keep Back and Continue accessible: they are part of the guided process.

### 3. Add the reusable guidance controller

```html
<script>
const form = document.getElementById("registrationForm");
const groups = [...form.querySelectorAll(".guided-field")];
const inputs = groups.map(group => group.querySelector("input"));
const guide = document.getElementById("guide");
const status = document.getElementById("status");
const back = document.getElementById("back");
const next = document.getElementById("next");

const steps = [
    "Enter your full name, then select Continue.",
    "Enter a valid email address, then select Continue.",
    "Use at least 12 characters, then select Continue.",
    "Repeat the password exactly, then select Create account."
];

let current = 0;
let busy = false;

function activate(index) {
    current = index;

    groups.forEach((group, i) => {
        group.disabled = i !== current;
        group.inert = i !== current;
        group.classList.toggle("active", i === current);
    });

    inputs.forEach(input => input.removeAttribute("aria-describedby"));
    inputs[current].setAttribute("aria-describedby", "guide");

    guide.textContent =
        `Step ${current + 1} of ${steps.length}: ${steps[current]}`;

    back.disabled = current === 0;
    next.textContent =
        current === steps.length - 1 ? "Create account" : "Continue";

    inputs[current].focus();
}

function validateCurrent() {
    const input = inputs[current];
    input.setCustomValidity("");

    if (input.id === "FullName" && !input.value.trim()) {
        input.setCustomValidity("Enter your name.");
    }

    if (input.id === "ConfirmPassword" &&
        input.value !== document.getElementById("Password").value) {
        input.setCustomValidity("Passwords must match.");
    }

    return input.reportValidity();
}

async function advance() {
    if (busy || !validateCurrent()) return;

    status.textContent = "";

    if (current < steps.length - 1) {
        activate(current + 1);
        return;
    }

    // Disabled controls are omitted from normal form submission.
    // Read every field explicitly, including disabled fields.
    const body = new URLSearchParams();
    inputs.forEach(input => body.set(input.name, input.value));

    const token = form.querySelector(
        'input[name="__RequestVerificationToken"]'
    );
    body.set(token.name, token.value);

    busy = true;
    back.disabled = next.disabled = true;
    groups[current].disabled = true;

    try {
        const response = await fetch(form.action, {
            method: "POST",
            body
        });

        const result = await response.json();

        if (!response.ok) {
            status.textContent =
                (result.errors ?? ["Registration failed."]).join(" ");
            return;
        }

        form.reset();
        status.textContent = "Account created successfully.";

        // Keep the completed registration locked.
        groups.forEach(group => {
            group.disabled = true;
            group.inert = true;
            group.classList.remove("active");
        });

        back.hidden = next.hidden = true;
    } catch {
        status.textContent =
            "Could not submit the registration. Please try again.";
    } finally {
        busy = false;
        if (!next.hidden) activate(current);
    }
}

back.addEventListener("click", () => {
    if (!busy && current > 0) activate(current - 1);
});

next.addEventListener("click", advance);

form.addEventListener("submit", event => {
    event.preventDefault();
    advance();
});

activate(0);
</script>
```

For a larger form, replace the instructions array with step objects containing field selectors, instructions, and optional custom validators.

### 4. Validate and create the account on the server

Your POST action should:

1. Use `[HttpPost]` and `[ValidateAntiForgeryToken]`.
2. Validate `ModelState`.
3. Call `UserManager<ApplicationUser>.CreateAsync(user, model.Password)`.
4. Return JSON success only if Identity successfully creates the account.
5. Return validation errors otherwise.

For example, the action’s validation branch can return:

```csharp
if (!ModelState.IsValid)
{
    return BadRequest(new
    {
        errors = ModelState.Values
            .SelectMany(value => value.Errors)
            .Select(error => error.ErrorMessage)
    });
}
```

Configure Identity’s password policy to match the instructions shown in the UI. For production, add field-specific server error mapping, email verification, rate limiting, and an accessible way to cancel or leave the guided process.

If step order is a **business requirement**, not just a UI preference, also track and validate permitted transitions on the server. Never trust a client-supplied current-step number.

